{"task_id": "HumanEval/75", "solution": "```python\ndef _is_prime(num):\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime. Helper for prime generation.\"\"\"\n    if num <= 1:\n        return False\n    if num == 2:\n        return True\n    if num % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= num:\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n\ndef _generate_primes_up_to(limit):\n    \"\"\"Generates a list of prime numbers up to a specified limit.\"\"\"\n    primes = []\n    for num in range(2, limit + 1):\n        if _is_prime(num):\n            primes.append(num)\n    return primes\n\ndef is_multiply_prime(a):\n    \"\"\"Determines if the number 'a' is a product of exactly three prime numbers.\"\"\"\n    if a < 8: # Handles 0, 1, negative, and numbers less than 8 (2*2*2 is minimum).\n        return False\n\n    primes = _generate_primes_up_to(a) # Generate primes up to 'a' as per design.\n    n = len(primes)\n\n    # Check all combinations of three primes (p1 * p2 * p3).\n    for i in range(n):\n        p1 = primes[i]\n        if p1 * p1 * p1 > a: # Optimization: if p1 cubed already exceeds 'a', no further products will match.\n            break\n        for j in range(i, n): # p2 >= p1 to allow repeated primes and avoid permutations.\n            p2 = primes[j]\n            if p1 * p2 * p2 > a: # Optimization: if p1 * p2 squared exceeds 'a', no further p3 will match.\n                break\n            for k in range(j, n): # p3 >= p2.\n                p3 = primes[k]\n                product = p1 * p2 * p3\n                if product == a:\n                    return True\n                if product > a: # Optimization: if product exceeds 'a', larger p3s won't match either.\n                    break\n\n    return False\n```"}
